{"answer":"There are 7 subsets. One example is {1,8}. Exhaustively: no singleton sums to 9; the four pairs are {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}; the three triples are {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, {2,3,4}; and any subset with at least four positive distinct elements has sum at least 1+2+3+4=10. Thus 4+3=7.","approaches":[{"description":"Classify subsets by cardinality, use the minimum possible sum for four distinct positive elements to eliminate all larger cardinalities, then list pairs and triples systematically.","id":"cardinality-casework","outcome":"candidate","reason":"It gives a finite disjoint partition of every possible subset and explicitly accounts for each case."},{"description":"Directly inspect all 256 subsets.","id":"brute-force-enumeration","outcome":"rejected","reason":"It is unnecessary for a self-contained exhaustive proof and would not explain the structural cutoff at four elements."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"Question premise: \"subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have sum 9\".","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"given-set-target","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The objects under consideration are subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} whose element-sum is 9.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in step-cardinality-bound.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["given-set-target"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"at-most-three-elements","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Any qualifying subset has at most three elements.","step_ids":["step-cardinality-bound"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in step-pairs and step-triples.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["at-most-three-elements"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"seven-total","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly seven subsets have sum 9.","step_ids":["step-pairs","step-triples","step-total"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-cardinality-bound","justification":"Among four distinct positive integers, the least possible sum is 1+2+3+4=10. Every member of the given set is positive, so a subset of four or more elements cannot sum to 9.","statement":"A qualifying subset has at most three elements."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-cardinality-bound"],"id":"step-singletons","justification":"Every available element is at most 8, so no singleton has sum 9; the empty subset has sum 0.","statement":"There are no qualifying subsets of size zero or one."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-cardinality-bound"],"id":"step-pairs","justification":"For a pair {a,b} with a<b and a+b=9, a can only be 1, 2, 3, or 4; these give respectively b=8,7,6,5. Hence the complete pair list is {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}.","statement":"There are exactly four qualifying pairs."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-cardinality-bound"],"id":"step-triples","justification":"Write a<b<c and a+b+c=9. If a=1, then b+c=8 and b is 2 or 3, giving {1,2,6} and {1,3,5}. If a=2, then b+c=7 and b=3, giving {2,3,4}. If a>=3, then a+b+c>=3+4+5=12. Thus these three triples are complete.","statement":"There are exactly three qualifying triples."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-singletons","step-pairs","step-triples"],"id":"step-total","justification":"The cases by subset cardinality are disjoint. Sizes zero and one contribute 0, size two contributes 4, size three contributes 3, and larger sizes are excluded by step-cardinality-bound; hence the total is 0+4+3=7.","statement":"The exhaustive count is seven."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
